# Test: a reload rotates the looking-glass certificate while a viewer is
# connected, and the listener keeps its socket.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-lg-pki-certificate AC-6. One commit repoints
# environment.looking-glass.certificate at a second entry of the same store. The
# open connection keeps carrying data, the listener address does not change, and
# the next handshake receives the new chain.
# PREVENTS: the shape the looking-glass server had before this spec. It built
# one tls.Config at construction and handed it to tls.NewListener, so the served
# certificate could not change without rebinding, and an operator renewing a
# certificate had to drop every viewer to publish it.
#
# The rotation fence is the daemon's own `sighup reload complete`, counted
# rather than searched, so the second reload of a run is not read as the first.
# The chain assertions are in the compiled fixture: the observer sandbox cannot
# drive a TLS handshake, and insecure-tls=true accepts a chain without reading
# it.
#
# The store here holds two leaves issued DIRECTLY by the root CA, and no
# intermediate. AC-5 and AC-6 are about which certificate a reload installs, not
# about how many the store holds. A chain-bearing store boots:
# test/plugin/lg-pki-certificate.ci carries one, and
# TestReloadInstallsEveryIntermediate (cmd/ze/hub/main_pki_test.go) pins the
# reload path.

option=timeout:value=120s

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture reload/lg-pki-reference-reload:timeout=90s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=OK: the next handshake receives the rotated chain
expect=stderr:contains=OK: the listener address is unchanged and the socket was never rebound
expect=stderr:contains=OK: the connection held across the rotation still carries data
expect=stderr:contains=sighup reload complete
